1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,640
Containers became widely popular around 2013–2015, 
following Docker’s launch in 2013 and Kubernetes’  

2
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:21,920
introduction in 2014. We can say that if a VM 
virtualizes hardware and runs a complete guest OS,  

3
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:28,800
then a container isolates one or more 
processes while sharing the host OS kernel.  

4
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:34,800
These processes are isolated from other processes, 
but their performance can still be affected by  

5
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:44,320
other workloads, as with VMs. The key difference 
is that containers also share the same kernel.

6
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:51,120
To isolate a set of processes, we must isolate 
resources and system views such as the filesystem,  

7
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:59,360
process IDs, user IDs, network interfaces, 
and IPC endpoints. That, as an example,  

8
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:03,200
allows for processes in different 
containers to have the same process  

9
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:10,960
ID. We may also define limits, quotas, 
or priorities for memory, CPU, and I/O.  

10
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:18,240
To do all this, we either use namespaces or 
cgroups. Namespaces isolate what processes  

11
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:25,200
can see, while cgroups control or 
account for the resources they use.

12
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:30,880
More concretely, if we focus on namespaces, a 
namespace is a kernel feature that limits or  

13
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:38,800
isolates what a process can see and access. A 
process belongs to one namespace of each type.  

14
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:43,760
New namespaces can be created when a process 
starts. As an example, I might want to start  

15
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:49,920
a process and associate it with a new mount 
namespace, so that the process gets a restricted  

16
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:56,320
view of the filesystem, for example only a 
specific folder and its subfolders. In that case,  

17
00:01:56,320 --> 00:02:03,360
I can create a new mount namespace and start the 
process in that namespace. A process can also join  

18
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:09,520
an existing namespace, of course, we do not need 
to create a new namespace for each new process.

19
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:15,680
Nonetheless, as said before, for memory, CPU, 
and I/O a namespace is not enough because we  

20
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:23,280
need a mechanism for the kernel to account for 
resource usage and enforce limits or priorities.

21
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:28,000
Going back to the file system example, 
a mount namespace isolates the set of  

22
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,440
filesystem mounts visible to a process. 
Two processes in different mount namespaces  

23
00:02:33,440 --> 00:02:37,440
may see different filesystems 
or different directory trees,  

24
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:43,200
even though they run on the same host. 
Container runtimes use mount namespaces  

25
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:49,440
together with other filesystem mechanisms to 
give each container its own root filesystem.

26
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:54,240
If we focus now on resources 
that require monitoring,  

27
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:59,680
what we have is that the kernel provides 
controllers for resource types such as CPU,  

28
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:04,800
memory, and I/O. For each resource, 
cgroups are organized in a tree and  

29
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:10,720
specify restrictions. A process can 
belong to one cgroup for each resource.

30
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:17,520
For example, we could have a CPU controller with 
a cgroup called c1 representing a 50% CPU limit  

31
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:22,480
(I am using percentages here only as a 
simplification) and a memory controller  

32
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:28,240
with a cgroup called m1 limiting 
usage to 2 GB. To limit processes A  

33
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:33,360
and B together to 50% of the CPU, 
we place both A and B in cgroup  

34
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:41,760
c1. To also limit process B to 2 GB of 
memory, we place B in memory cgroup m1.

35
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:47,760
So, why are cgroups hierarchical, and 
why are there multiple controllers?

36
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:52,480
The hierarchy allows limits and resource 
distribution to be defined at several  

37
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:59,360
levels. Multiple controllers exist because 
CPU time, CPU placement, memory, I/O,  

38
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:04,800
and process counts require different 
accounting and enforcement mechanisms.

39
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:11,600
In the previous example, A and B together received 
50% of the CPU. To divide that allocation further,  

40
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:19,840
for example, 80% for A and 20% for B, we can 
create two child cgroups below the 50% group.

41
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:26,000
Note that some controllers affect aspects 
of execution that are related. For instance,  

42
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:32,560
the CPU controller manages CPU time, while the 
cpuset controller determines which CPUs a process  

43
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:39,200
may use. On a four-core system, restricting A and 
B to two cores prevents them from using more than  

44
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:47,920
two cores simultaneously, so in a sense, that 
also limits A and B to maximum 50% of the CPU.

45
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:52,640
Note also that different restrictions may 
conflict. For example, a process may belong  

46
00:04:52,640 --> 00:04:59,760
to a cpuset cgroup that permits only core 1, 
while one of its threads is pinned to core 2.  

47
00:04:59,760 --> 00:05:05,280
The OS enforces the intersection of these 
restrictions. Since the thread has no  

48
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:12,160
eligible CPU, it cannot be scheduled 
until one of the restrictions changes.

49
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:17,760
As we said, different controllers 
manage different resources. For example,  

50
00:05:17,760 --> 00:05:21,920
the CPU controller manages CPU 
bandwidth or relative weight,  

51
00:05:21,920 --> 00:05:28,560
cpuset controls CPU and NUMA placement, 
memory controls memory usage, and pids  

52
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:37,120
limits the number of processes or tasks. The 
devices controller manages device access.

53
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:42,160
Please note that Linux supports two 
main versions of the cgroup interface.

54
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:45,920
In cgroup v1, controllers 
can use separate hierarchies,  

55
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:51,360
so a process may belong to one cgroup for 
CPU control and another for memory control.

56
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:57,600
Cgroup v2 uses one unified hierarchy. A process 
belongs to one cgroup in that hierarchy,  

57
00:05:57,600 --> 00:06:02,000
and the relevant controllers are enabled 
for that part of the tree. This provides  

58
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:07,440
a more consistent relationship between 
processes, parent groups, and resource limits.

59
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:11,520
The following examples mix both 
versions because their purpose is to  

60
00:06:11,520 --> 00:06:17,600
illustrate what cgroups can do rather 
than the exact configuration interface.

61
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:22,480
Let’s look at a more thorough 
example for the CPU controller.  

62
00:06:22,480 --> 00:06:30,720
Each cgroup is assigned a number of shares. 
Typically, system.slice contains system services,  

63
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:37,280
user.slice contains user processes, and 
machine.slice contains VMs and containers.

64
00:06:37,280 --> 00:06:43,600
In this example, these three groups and G1 
have equal shares. Note that shares matter only  

65
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:49,760
during contention, because they define relative 
fairness, not fixed limits. Under contention,  

66
00:06:49,760 --> 00:06:55,840
G1 therefore receives approximately 
one quarter of the available CPU time.

67
00:06:55,840 --> 00:07:04,800
Within G1, G1.A has 1,000 
shares and G1.B has 10,000.  

68
00:07:04,800 --> 00:07:12,160
G1.A therefore receives approximately 
one eleventh of G1’s allocation,  

69
00:07:12,160 --> 00:07:20,400
or about 1/44 (so around 2.3%) of the 
total CPU capacity under full contention.

70
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:28,160
If you want to enforce a strict max, you can 
do that instead with cpu.max in cgroup v2.

71
00:07:29,760 --> 00:07:35,280
Different controllers use different types 
of thresholds. Memory limits, for example,  

72
00:07:35,280 --> 00:07:44,080
are commonly expressed as absolute byte values.
Here, G2.1 is limited to 1 GB and G2.2 to 1.5  

73
00:07:44,080 --> 00:07:51,520
GB. Together, however, they cannot use more 
than the 2 GB limit of their parent, G2.

74
00:07:51,520 --> 00:07:58,400
A child cgroup is constrained by both 
its own limit and its ancestors’ limits.

75
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:04,080
If you want, you can check the 
membership of each process.

76
00:08:04,080 --> 00:08:10,880
This file shows the cgroup membership of 
process 4042126. Each line identifies a  

77
00:08:10,880 --> 00:08:16,160
cgroup hierarchy or controller and the path 
of the cgroup to which the process belongs.

78
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:19,600
If you want to check the 
cgroup user-slice for blkio,  

79
00:08:20,640 --> 00:08:29,360
then this directory contains the cgroup v1 blkio 
controller files for user.slice. For example,  

80
00:08:29,360 --> 00:08:35,840
reading the highlighted file shows the accumulated 
bytes transferred by devices for this cgroup.

